—'''Babbitting jig''', {{smaller|a tool used to hold bearing-boxes in position about the journals of shafts, etc., while any box-lining metal, as the Babbitt, is being poured in.}}—'''Babbitting ladle''', {{smaller|an iron ladle used to pour the Babbitt metal or any antifriction alloy upon the bearings of machinery.}}

'''Babbitt metal.''' See ''metal.''

'''babblative''' (bab′la̤-tiv), ''a.'' [Formerly also ''bablative''; < ''babble'' + ''-ative.'' Cf. ''talkative.''] Given to babbling.
{{smaller block|Argumentative, ''babblative'', and unpleasant to me.
{{right|''Carlyle'', in Froude, I. 119.}}}}

'''babble''' (bab′l), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''babbled'', ppr. ''babbling.'' [< ME. ''babelen'', ''bablen'' = D. ''babbelen'' = LG. ''babbelen'' = Icel. ''babbla'' = Dan. ''bable'' = G. ''bappeln'', ''bappern'', babble (cf. F. ''babiller'', chatter); all perhaps imitative, with freq. suffix ''-l'', from the redupl. syllable ''ba'': see ''ba''<sup>2</sup>.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To utter words imperfectly or indistinctly, as children do; prattle; jabber.
{{smaller block|<poem>I ''babbled'' for you, as babies for the moon,
Vague brightness. {{right|''Tennyson'', Princess, iv.}}</poem>}}
'''2.''' To talk idly, irrationally, or thoughtlessly; chatter or prate heedlessly or mischievously.
{{smaller block|A ''babbled'' of green fields. {{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., ii. 3.}}
<poem>The people, when they met, . . .
Began to scoff and jeer and ''babble'' of him.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Geraint.}}}}
'''3.''' To make a continuous murmuring sound, as a stream; repeat a sound frequently and indistinctly.
{{smaller block|The ''babbling'' echo mocks the hounds.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of A., ii. 3.}}
The ''babbling'' runnel crispeth. {{right|''Tennyson'', Claribel.}}}}

